+## Frame-Averaged (Yaw-Equivariant) Conditioning
+
+For a mono RIR, rigidly rotating a whole scene -- geometry, source and listener together -- about
+the vertical axis leaves the ground-truth response unchanged, yet the standard conditioning is not
+invariant to that rotation: the absolute pose vectors and the equirectangular depth panorama both
+move. Setting `training.cond_method` to `"fa_invariant"` makes the conditioning exactly invariant
+along two independent paths. The source and context poses become cylindrical features
+`(r, z, dphi)`, where `dphi` is a pose's azimuth measured *relative* to the target source's
+azimuth; a rigid yaw shifts every azimuth by the same amount, so these features are invariant at
+**any** angle. The two DINOv3 depth-panorama conditioners (`source_vit`, `context_poses_vit`) are
+instead **frame-averaged** over the C4 orbit: per angle the depth map is rolled horizontally and
+the `*_vit` poses are rotated *together*, and the conditioner outputs are averaged. At the panorama
+width used here (512 columns) 90 degrees is exactly 128 columns, so the roll is a lossless integer
+shift and the average is exactly invariant on that subgroup. All other conditioners run once.
+
+The configuration is `src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json`: the baseline
+`FLAC_AR.json` plus exactly four keys -- `training.cond_method`, `training.frame_avg_angles`, and
+`gradient_checkpointing` on each of the two ViT conditioners. Both configs pin the DINOv3 backbone
+to an immutable Hub snapshot (`revision`). The orbit is configurable through
+`training.frame_avg_angles` (degrees): the first angle must be `0` (the identity pass), and angles
+that divide the panorama width evenly -- as the default `[0, 90, 180, 270]` does -- are exact;
+any other angle is quantised to the nearest column.
+
+### Training
+
+```bash
+python train.py \
+--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
+--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json \
+--pretransform-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/VAE.safetensors \
+--max-steps 67500 --batch-size 32 --accum-batches 1 --seed 42 \
+--num-gpus 2 --strategy ddp_find_unused_parameters_true --sync-batchnorm true \
+--checkpoint-every 2500 \
+--name FLAC_FA --experiment-name FA_training --save-dir ./outputs_FLAC
+```
+
+`--sync-batchnorm true` synchronises BatchNorm statistics across ranks, so the effective BatchNorm
+batch stays at 32 x 2 = 64 on two GPUs; it requires `--num-gpus >= 2` and fails closed otherwise,
+and gradient accumulation is no substitute (BatchNorm only ever sees one micro-batch).
+`gradient_checkpointing` in the config trades backward-pass recompute for the activation memory the
+four-angle orbit costs, which is what lets a micro-batch of 32 fit on a 48 GB card. Because the ViT
+conditioners are re-run per orbit angle, each optimizer step is correspondingly slower than the
+standard recipe.
+
+### Evaluation
+
+```bash
+python eval_FLAC.py \
+--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json \
+--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_unseeneval.json \
+--ckpt-path /path/to/FLAC_FA/ckpt \
+--cond-autocast bf16 --steps 1 --cfg-scale 1.0 --seed 42
+```
+
+`--cond-method` **defaults to inheriting the method the checkpoint was trained with**, read from the
+`training` block embedded in the checkpoint (or, for a bare exported checkpoint, from
+`--model-config`); `--frame-avg-angles` inherits the trained orbit the same way. A value that
+contradicts the trained protocol is refused unless `--allow-conditioning-override` is passed, and an
+overriding run is marked as such in its output record. The guard exists because the failure mode is
+silent: evaluating frame-averaged weights under vanilla conditioning -- or vanilla weights under
+frame-averaged conditioning -- produces plausible-looking numbers, wrong in both directions.
+
+`--cond-autocast bf16` runs the conditioning call in bfloat16, matching the `bf16-mixed` precision
+the model trains under. The CLI default is `default` (the per-device autocast dtype, i.e. fp16 on
+CUDA) and the choice measurably shifts the metrics, so comparable runs must all pass it explicitly.
+
+### Rotation-robustness check
+
+`--rotate-deg 90` physically rotates the conditioning -- depth panorama and poses together -- before
+it is encoded, while the target RIR stays fixed; the rotation is applied first and the conditioning
+method on top of it. An `fa_invariant` model should return unchanged metrics at the C4 angles,
+because its conditioning is invariant there by construction. Output files pick up a `_rot<deg>`
+suffix (alongside a `_fa_invariant_a<n>` method suffix), so the runs of a sweep never overwrite
+each other.
+
+### Supported inference API
+
+`train.py`, `eval_FLAC.py` and `eval_pl.py` all build their conditioning through one shared dispatch
+function, `src.data.yaw_rotation.dispatch_conditioning`, so a checkpoint's method is honoured
+identically wherever it is run. The lower-level `generate_diffusion_cond` /
+`ConditionedDiffusionModelWrapper.generate()` path does **not** go through that dispatch: given raw
+`conditioning` it calls the conditioner directly, which is the vanilla path. To drive a
+frame-averaged model through it, precompute the conditioning and pass it as tensors instead:
+
+```python
+from src.data.yaw_rotation import invariant_conditioning
+
+cond = invariant_conditioning(model.conditioner, metadata, device)
+audio = model.generate(steps=1, cfg_scale=1.0, conditioning_tensors=cond, sample_size=10240)
+```
+
+
