   170	
   171	## Frame-Averaged (Yaw-Equivariant) Conditioning
   172	
   173	For a mono RIR, rigidly rotating a whole scene -- geometry, source and listener together -- about
   174	the vertical axis leaves the ground-truth response unchanged, yet the standard conditioning is not
   175	invariant to that rotation: the absolute pose vectors and the equirectangular depth panorama both
   176	move. Setting `training.cond_method` to `"fa_invariant"` makes the conditioning exactly invariant
   177	along two independent paths. The source and context poses become cylindrical features
   178	`(r, z, dphi)`, where `dphi` is a pose's azimuth measured *relative* to the target source's
   179	azimuth; a rigid yaw shifts every azimuth by the same amount, so these features are invariant at
   180	**any** angle. The two DINOv3 depth-panorama conditioners (`source_vit`, `context_poses_vit`) are
   181	instead **frame-averaged** over the C4 orbit: per angle the depth map is rolled horizontally and
   182	the `*_vit` poses are rotated *together*, and the conditioner outputs are averaged. At the panorama
   183	width used here (512 columns) 90 degrees is exactly 128 columns, so the roll is a lossless integer
   184	shift and the average is exactly invariant on that subgroup. All other conditioners run once.
   185	
   186	The configuration is `src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json`: the baseline
   187	`FLAC_AR.json` plus exactly four keys -- `training.cond_method`, `training.frame_avg_angles`, and
   188	`gradient_checkpointing` on each of the two ViT conditioners. Both configs pin the DINOv3 backbone
   189	to an immutable Hub snapshot (`revision`). The orbit is configurable through
   190	`training.frame_avg_angles` (degrees): the first angle must be `0` (the identity pass), and angles
   191	that divide the panorama width evenly -- as the default `[0, 90, 180, 270]` does -- are exact;
   192	any other angle is quantised to the nearest column.
   193	
   194	### Training
   195	
   196	```bash
   197	python train.py \
   198	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
   199	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json \
   200	--pretransform-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/VAE.safetensors \
   201	--max-steps 67500 --batch-size 32 --accum-batches 1 --seed 42 \
   202	--num-gpus 2 --strategy ddp_find_unused_parameters_true --sync-batchnorm true \
   203	--checkpoint-every 2500 \
   204	--name FLAC_FA --experiment-name FA_training --save-dir ./outputs_FLAC
   205	```
   206	
   207	`--sync-batchnorm true` synchronises BatchNorm statistics across ranks, so the effective BatchNorm
   208	batch stays at 32 x 2 = 64 on two GPUs; it requires `--num-gpus >= 2` and fails closed otherwise,
   209	and gradient accumulation is no substitute (BatchNorm only ever sees one micro-batch).
   210	`gradient_checkpointing` in the config trades backward-pass recompute for the activation memory the
   211	four-angle orbit costs, which is what lets a micro-batch of 32 fit on a 48 GB card. Because the ViT
   212	conditioners are re-run per orbit angle, each optimizer step is correspondingly slower than the
   213	standard recipe.
   214	
