   233	`--cond-autocast bf16` runs the conditioning call in bfloat16, matching the `bf16-mixed` precision
   234	the model trains under. The CLI default is `default` (the per-device autocast dtype, i.e. fp16 on
   235	CUDA) and the choice measurably shifts the metrics, so comparable runs must all pass it explicitly.
   236	
   237	### Rotation-robustness check
   238	
   239	`--rotate-deg 90` physically rotates the conditioning -- depth panorama and poses together -- before
   240	it is encoded, while the target RIR stays fixed; the rotation is applied first and the conditioning
   241	method on top of it. An `fa_invariant` model should return unchanged metrics at the C4 angles,
   242	because its conditioning is invariant there by construction. Output files pick up a `_rot<deg>`
   243	suffix (alongside a `_fa_invariant_a<n>` method suffix), so the runs of a sweep never overwrite
   244	each other.
   245	
   246	### Supported inference API
   247	
   248	`train.py`, `eval_FLAC.py` and `eval_pl.py` all build their conditioning through one shared dispatch
   249	function, `src.data.yaw_rotation.dispatch_conditioning`, so a checkpoint's method is honoured
   250	identically wherever it is run. The lower-level `generate_diffusion_cond` /
   251	`ConditionedDiffusionModelWrapper.generate()` path does **not** go through that dispatch: given raw
   252	`conditioning` it calls the conditioner directly, which is the vanilla path. To drive a
   253	frame-averaged model through it, precompute the conditioning and pass it as tensors instead:
   254	
   255	```python
   256	from src.data.yaw_rotation import invariant_conditioning
   257	
   258	cond = invariant_conditioning(model.conditioner, metadata, device)
   259	audio = model.generate(steps=1, cfg_scale=1.0, conditioning_tensors=cond, sample_size=10240)
   260	```
